{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310672,"nonce":"tlDYqIMQFq4wS63CV55ntBas4V4=","title":"Under RAYZOR_POOL=1 RAYZOR_POOL_STATS=1, the tensor pool reports hits=0 pushes=0 across 3029-9081 misses per llama-chat run. Transient ...","message":"The shape-class bucket pool ([[project-tensor-pool]], commit 7222541) is structurally correct and unit-tested (16/16 + 8×1000 stress) but **dead infrastructure on the real workload**. With `RAYZOR_POOL=1 RAYZOR_POOL_STATS=1`, both llama-chat (24-token) and tiny-transformer report:\n\n```\n[RAYZOR pool] hits=0 misses=3029-9081 pushes=0 evictions=0 peak_per_bucket=0 peak_bytes=0MB\n```\n\n`try_pop` is exercised on every owning alloc, but `rayzor_tensor_free` is never invoked for transient activation tensors created during the forward pass. The bucket store stays empty; every acquire is a cold miss.\n\n**Why it matters:** the pool's value proposition is steady-state alloc reduction. With pushes=0 the pool adds a per-alloc Mutex+HashMap lookup tax for zero observable benefit. Default-off bench matches pre-pool baseline within noise; `RAYZOR_POOL=1` doesn't move it either. Both costs are within noise but neither delivers the headline win.\n\n**Root causes (suspected):**\n\n1. **KV-cache reference holding**: KV-cache tensors per (layer, head) slot are append-only buffers that the cache module retains a reference to for the next token. Those tensors are intentionally not freed per token — they accumulate context. This is correct behaviour, not a bug; they're not pool candidates.\n\n2. **Drop-analysis / `InsertFreePass` gap on transient ops**: outputs of `binop_with_out`, `unary_with_out`, `bmm`, `matmul`, `matmul_t`, `scale`, RMSNorm, softmax — these flow through the transformer block per token and SHOULD be released when their last use completes. The audit suggests `InsertFreePass` is not emitting frees for many of these. Same class of bug as [[bugs-cleanup-scopes-frees-captures]] (FIXED for explicit return paths) and [[bugs-mark-escaping-incomplete]] (FIXED for composite escape detection). May need a third sweep specifically for forward-pass intermediate tensors.\n\n3. **`LlamaModel.hx:65` defensive clone holding**: per [[bugs-addinto-rebind-clone-drop-failure]], the loop holds `var hIn = h.clone()` which becomes a per-iteration allocation that the analyser must release. This is one of the visible drop-analysis failures.\n\n**How to investigate:**\n\n1. Instrument `rayzor_tensor_free` to log every (caller, shape, dtype) tuple with `RAYZOR_FREE_LOG=1`. Run a 4-token llama-chat. Diff against `RAYZOR_ALLOC_HISTOGRAM=1` (already exists per the workflow). Every shape in the alloc histogram that does NOT appear in the free log is a drop-analysis gap.\n\n2. For each missing free shape, identify the producing kernel (`bmm`, `scale`, etc.) and check whether the producer's caller in nue/* has a clear \"last use\" point. If yes, the gap is in drop-analysis; if no, the binding is still live (and would need restructuring or `.addInto()`-style in-place semantics).\n\n3. Cross-reference with `InsertFreePass` source in `compiler/src/ir/insert_free_pass.rs`. Look at how `binop_with_out` outputs are handled — is there a free emission at the use's scope exit?\n\n**Until fixed:**\n\n- Pool ships opt-in via `RAYZOR_POOL=1` (default disabled). Documented in commit 7222541.\n- Performance claim in any future pool-related commit must say \"plumbed-but-dormant\" or \"infrastructure-only\" — NOT \"removes alloc churn from the hot loop\".\n- `RAYZOR_TENSOR_POOL_POISON=1` is the diagnostic: any UAF that would otherwise surface as silent value corruption (because the freed buffer stays parked) shows up as `0xCDCDCDCD` sentinel patterns.\n\n**Highest-leverage single test case** for the drop-analysis fix: a 24-token llama-chat under `RAYZOR_POOL=1 RAYZOR_POOL_STATS=1` should show `hits \u003e 0` and `pushes \u003e 0` after the gap is closed. The fix should also unblock the `LlamaModel.hx:65` clone drop (~3 MB allocator churn / 24-token decode) — see [[bugs-addinto-rebind-clone-drop-failure]].\n\n**BIGGEST WIN of the arc: StringBuf rewritten on rayzor.Bytes (9a67f9f0).** The vanilla Haxe StringBuf\n(`b += x`) leaked ONE intermediate String PER APPEND (no GC + immutable strings) — O(n) per built string;\nthe WordPiece tokenizer builds every candidate through it. New impl: mutable Bytes buffer (grow=double+\nblit+free old), UTF-8 addChar in place, add/addSub bulk-copy via transient Bytes.ofString (freed), toString\n= sub+toString+free sub. haxe-std is DISK-RESOLVED (no rust rebuild — but MUST clear .rayzor caches).\nVerified tok 32/32 EXACT both platforms, textgold bit-identical, llama coherent. **Leak 84→42 MB/300k\ntensor-allocs (~10.8→5.4 KB/sentence).** Also: `String.length`/charCodeAt are BYTE-based in rayzor\n(charCodeAt = raw byte read) so byte-loop copies are exact; StringBuf.length semantics unchanged.\n**String-tracking machinery landed (debcedb3), retention the one unsound piece (GATED OFF):**\n- 10 VERIFIED Box-fresh extern string producers tracked (also copy_only: inputs copied). **value_to_string_\n  by_tag EXCLUDED — tag-5 returns the INPUT pointer** (freeing it = freeing the caller's string).\n  haxe_string_free Box-reclaims the header → strings get ONLY the free call, never IrInstruction::Free.\n- returns_fresh_strings fixpoint + stringmap_get/exists verified read-only (map stores RUST-string copies).\n- **Alias holes closed: SsaBarrier + Select now propagate in build_derived_set** (store-through-barrier'd-\n  alias was invisible → freed-while-stored).\n- **compute_param_retention (optimistic per-param fixpoint) is UNSOUND somewhere: RZT_PARAM_RETENTION=1 →\n  tok 4/32, textgold 0.665 (strings stored into result structs get freed — 27 str-ins in `parameters`,\n  whose NamedTensor name strings must live).** Same-cosine signature as the arg0 bug. RZT_DBG_RETENTION=1\n  dumps verdicts: `set [1,2]`, `addSpecial [1,2]`, `new [1]` etc — names AMBIGUOUS across classes (many\n  `set`/`new`), and Int params in the clear-list are noise (harmless). NEXT BISECT: pick ONE bad insertion\n  (e.g. parameters' name string), dump that function + the ctor/helper it calls with RAYZOR_DUMP_MIR, find\n  which cleared (callee,pos) the store hides behind — suspect a field-store lowered to something the\n  Store{value} sink misses (coercion call? interface dispatch? CallIndirect-through-vtable = caught...).\n  Retention default OFF = all green (tok 32/32, textgold 0.9999983, int8, llama 40.8). strfree counter\n  (RZT_DBG_STRFREE=1) proves string frees fire on the default path. Leak unchanged vs post-StringBuf until\n  retention is sound (candidates escape into @lookup).\n\nDelivered the interprocedural piece, verified everywhere (NUC textgold 0.9999983, maskTest PASS, int8\n0.9920855, llama 41.6 tok/s coherent; ~1.8 frees/encode fire in the bench hot loop — RZT_DBG_ARRFREE=1\ncounter in haxe_array_free proves it; RZT_DBG_ARR=1 lists insertion sites at compile time):\n- **returns_fresh_array fixpoint** (module-wide): fn is fresh iff every value-return is covered by a local\n  array-header malloc OR a fresh-call dest whose ONLY escape is the return + not freed locally. Propagates\n  through wrappers (embedText→embed). Callers treat fresh-call dests as OWNED arrays: freed at returns\n  (entry-block) or end-of-defining-block when confined (per-iteration discard — benchMode's `embedText(s)`).\n- **CRITICAL UAF LESSON (cost a cosine collapse to 0.665, fixed same session): \"safe\" accessor calls are\n  safe for the RECEIVER (arg0) ONLY.** The original whitelist skipped ALL args → a pointer PUSHED AS A VALUE\n  into another array (array_push(other, OUR_PTR)) was freed while stored inside it. Fix = args beyond arg0\n  of a safe accessor still escape (except verified copy_only fns). Also instruction-level `Return` now\n  counts as an escape (was terminator-only).\n- Measured: leak 89→84 MB per 300k tensor-allocs — arrays are a MINORITY of the residual (~1.6KB/encode\n  recovered). **The dominant remaining category = HAXE STRINGS (~10KB/encode, WordPiece candidates).**\nSTRINGS DESIGN MAP (verified facts, for the next session):\n- HaxeString = {ptr,len,cap} Box'd header + owned buffer. TWO runtime families: sret (haxe_string_substr/\n  substring: caller-slot out, fresh buffer) and **ptr-return (what the MIR actually calls): haxe_string_lower/\n  char_at_ptr/substr_ptr/concat/from_* — VERIFIED Box::into_raw fresh on every path (lower + char_at_ptr read;\n  verify the rest before whitelisting).** haxe_string_free frees buffer AND Box::from_raw's the header →\n  emit ONLY the free call for strings, NEVER IrInstruction::Free (and NEVER on an sret slot — stack!).\n- encodeUtf8 (the tokenizer's candidate producer) = StringBuf pattern: malloc'd builder + @addChar loop +\n  single `$32 = call @toString; ret $32` → chains fresh via the fixpoint IF StringBuf.toString's own final\n  producer is whitelisted (toString is a MODULE fn — the per-function-body analysis handles the generic name).\n- **BLOCKER for the hot-path win: wordPiece passes candidates to `@lookup` — a MODULE wrapper fn, NOT an\n  inlined haxe_stringmap_exists** → candidates escape into the call. Freeing them needs a PARAM-RETENTION\n  fixpoint (param non-retaining iff it flows only into copy_only/receiver-safe positions + not stored/\n  returned) — the real unlock, on top of: ext-producer whitelist verification, returns_fresh_strings fixpoint,\n  read-only consumers into copy_only (length/compare/char_code_at_ptr/index_of_ptr/starts_with/stringmap_get/\n  exists — get/exists verified read-key-only), haxe_string_free extern declaration. Each = UAF risk; full\n  battery (BERT+llama) per step. MIR fn-id→name archaeology across module copies is UNRELIABLE — use the\n  RZT_DBG_* compiler prints instead.\n\nGot local non-escaping Haxe arrays freeing, verified UAF-safe on BERT+llama. Key facts learned by MIR trace +\na compiler debug print (RZT_DBG_FNS in classify_func / RZT_DBG_ARR in insert_free_for_function, both removed):\n- **A HaxeArray = a `malloc`'d 32-byte header (ptr/len/cap/elem) + a heap buffer** grown by haxe_array_push in\n  the runtime. NO haxe_array_new call — header is `$12 = call malloc(32)` then inline field stores. So the\n  header IS a tracked malloc; buffer freed by haxe_array_free (declared as extern — nue never calls it).\n- **It escaped via a CHAIN of loop phis** (fill loop `$29=phi[$12,$29]`, then MUTUALLY-RECURSIVE mean-pool phis\n  `$50=phi[$44,$56]`,`$56=phi[$50,$96]`). Fix = OPTIMISTIC build_derived_set (add a phi if ANY incoming is\n  derived — reaches through mutual recursion; an \"all incoming\" rule DEADLOCKS on `$50↔$56`), and\n  pointer_escapes flags a phi only if it merges a value NOT derived (real foreign merge). This is a GENERAL\n  win (loop-carried non-escaping allocs of any kind now free). Identify arrays = a malloc whose result/derived/\n  phi-alias is arg0 of a NON-RETAINING array op (scalar get_f64/set_f64/push_f64/pop/length/contains/index_of;\n  NOT iterator/`_ptr`/map/filter/sort/closures). Emit haxe_array_free THEN Free at scope exit. alloc-only\n  identification excludes borrowed params/fields (no double-free).\n- **BUT measurable BERT-leak impact ≈ ZERO**: `acc` is only ~3 KB/encode. The residual ~30 KB/encode is\n  (a) ESCAPING/returned arrays — the tokenizer `ids` (returned up, discarded late), the `[for j] embedding\n  return array (bench discards it; a server would free-late); (b) Haxe STRINGS — WordPiece substrings (a whole\n  separate category, haxe_string_*, untouched). NEXT PIECES: caller-side drop of received/discarded array\n  RETURN VALUES (needs interprocedural \"does callee retain arg\" — like the copy_only whitelist but general),\n  and string-lifetime. Both bigger. The committed work is the correct, safe FOUNDATION + a general phi fix.\n\nPushed further on the branch (main untouched). Built + tested TWO insert_free extensions, BOTH verified\nUAF-safe on BERT (goldens unchanged) but BOTH ZERO leak effect — each ruled out a hypothesis:\n1. copy_only whitelist (2f553710): args to rayzor_tensor_from_array don't escape. No effect — arrays aren't\n   tracked at all. Also: fromArray takes the array's LOADED data-buffer ptr (acc.ptr) + len, NOT the header,\n   so the header never even flows to fromArray as a pointer arg.\n2. array-free scaffolding (33951d68): track haxe_array_new → arg0 header, array_safe_ids escape whitelist\n   (scalar ops; NOT iterator/`_ptr`/closures), emit haxe_array_free (null-safe, runtime/haxe_array.rs:759)\n   at scope exit for non-escaping headers. No effect — **the MIR has NO haxe_array_new / array_new CALL**:\n   Haxe arrays are created INLINE (an `alloc` header + inline field stores; the data buffer is malloc'd on the\n   first haxe_array_push INSIDE the runtime). MIR array ops seen: @array_push, @array_length, @haxe_array_get_f64,\n   @haxe_array_push_f64, @haxe_array_set_f64/i64/null (mixed @array_* / @haxe_array_* spelling).\nREAL REMAINING STEP (focused session): identify arrays by USAGE not by a constructor call — an owned local\narray = an `alloc` dest used as arg0 of a safe array op (the alloc-dest restriction is load-bearing: it excludes\nborrowed param/field arrays, else scope-exit free = double-free). The header alloc was NOT trivially spotted in\nthe embed MIR (`$27 = alloc i64` is 8B; HaxeArray is 32B) — needs a real trace of what defines array_push's\narg0. Then the committed escape-analysis + haxe_array_free-at-scope-exit plumbing applies. Aliasing: struct-copy\ncaught by the MemCopy escape arm, pointer-copy by the derived set. MUST verify on BERT+llama+full test suite.\nDo NOT re-try copy_only-alone or haxe_array_new-tracking — both proven no-ops here.\n\nMerged feat/bert-embed-server → main (FF, 12 commits, ahead 33 of origin, NOT pushed). Then started the\nescape-analysis fix on branch feat/haxe-escape-analysis. LANDED 2f553710: `copy_only_ids` whitelist in\ninsert_free.rs pointer_escapes — args to a verified copy-only callee (rayzor_tensor_from_array copies the\nArray\u003cFloat\u003e element-by-element into a fresh tensor buf, retains nothing) no longer count as escapes. BERT\ngoldens unchanged; **but ZERO leak effect** and here's why (fully traced):\n**insert_free tracks ONLY malloc/haxe_type_create_*/rayzor_anon_new — NOT Haxe arrays.** A Haxe array =\na stack-slot HEADER written by `haxe_array_new(out, elem_size)` (runtime/haxe_array.rs:29) + a HEAP data\nbuffer grown via std::alloc in haxe_array_push/resize; freed by `haxe_array_free` (haxe_array.rs:759,\n`dealloc(arr.ptr, layout)`). **haxe_array_free is emitted 0 times → every array's data buffer leaks.** The\ndata buffer is malloc'd INSIDE the runtime array ops, invisible to insert_free's MIR-level malloc tracking.\nREMAINING WORK to make it bite (the delicate part): (1) track `haxe_array_new` calls (arg0 `out` = the array\nheader) as a new alloc category; (2) whitelist the NON-retaining array ops as safe-for-the-receiver\n(array_push/array_length/array_get/set/map/filter/concat/slice…) but NOT array_iterator/array_kv_iterator\n(the iterator HOLDS the array → retains it → escape); (3) run pointer_escapes (now incl. the copy_only\nwhitelist); (4) emit `haxe_array_free(header)` at scope exit for non-escaping arrays. RISK = high (UAF across\nALL nue array code); needs per-op retain analysis + FULL-suite verification (BERT+llama+tests), not just BERT.\nThe [[bugs_float_conditional_reassign_boxes]] \u0026 string leaks are the same class. This is the \"Haxe object\nlifetime\" project — sizable + risky; the whitelist is a safe committed foundation for it.\n\nBuilt RZT_LEAK_STATS=1 (rayzor-tensors/tensor.rs): NET owning-tensor live bytes (alloc_tensor_with_zero_policy\n+=, rayzor_tensor_free owns_data path -=; views carry no data) printed vs process RSS every 20k allocs.\nVERDICT across a long encode loop: **tensor_net_live FLAT at 90.4 MB while RSS climbs 457→548 MB** → the\nresidual ~0.5-1.2 MB/iter is ENTIRELY NON-TENSOR. So the tensor frees (974fde4f) are complete + balanced (no\ntensor leak, no missed free, no Rust-side tensor scratch leak) — the residual is Haxe arrays/strings: the\nno-GC + CONSERVATIVE-ESCAPE limitation. Sub-causes: (1) genuinely-escaping return arrays (`embedText` returns\n`[for j in 0...dim]`, discarded by the bench / freed-late by a server); (2) FALSELY-escaping args — `acc` in\n`embed()` is passed to `Tensor.fromArray` which COPIES it, but insert_free.rs pointer_escapes conservatively\nmarks ANY call-arg as escaping → never freed; (3) multi-block tokenizer temps. **Haxe arrays have NO manual\n`.free()` (only tensors do) so this CANNOT be fixed in nue code — it is a COMPILER fix**: interprocedural\nescape analysis (whitelist copying callees like fromArray, à la anon_safe_ids), caller-side drop of discarded\ncall results, and post-dominance freeing of multi-block temps — collectively a large effort, NOT a targeted\npatch. My single-block-confined insert_free extension (built+verified UAF-safe on BERT+llama, REVERTED) moved\nthis leak by ZERO, confirming it's multi-block/escaping. GC or the above escape-analysis suite is the real fix.\nGOTCHA: rayzor-tensors is a cdylib PLUGIN (crate-type cdylib+rlib, workspace member NOT a rayzor dep); bert\nloads librayzor_tensors.so via [build] native-libs, so `-p rayzor` does NOT rebuild it — instrument/fix needs\n`cargo build -p rayzor-tensors` to refresh the .so.\n\nKEY REFRAME: for TENSORS this is NOT a compiler/InsertFreePass gap — **tensors are manually freed BY\nDESIGN**. `Tensor` is `@:derive([Clone])` (NOT Drop) with an explicit `free()`; insert_free.rs tracks only\nmalloc/haxe_type_create/rayzor_anon (line 136, \"Inner-block allocs: skip for now\" is about those, not\ntensors), and the hir_to_mir drop machinery (cleanup_all_scopes) fires ONLY for `@:derive(Drop)` classes.\nSo nothing auto-frees a `Tensor` — the nue code must call `.free()` at last use. TransformerBlock/\nforwardPostNorm already did this diligently; the leak was the sublayers + encode driver NOT freeing:\nMultiHeadAttention.forward/forwardMasked (x clones, q/k/v projections, reshape/permute VIEWS, bmm/scale/\nsoftmax outputs — the big [heads,seq,seq]), GeluFFN (the [seq,intermediate] up + gelu), LayerNorm\n(`x.layerNorm(eps)` chained temp — ~14/encode; mul() doesn't free its receiver), BertModel.encode\n(embedding-stack h0/lookups/h1/h2 + the old `hBlocks` overwritten each layer).\nFIX = bind every intermediate + `.free()` after the independent output is computed (refcount-protected, so\nrelease order doesn't matter and views release their parent). RESULT: NUC ~70 → ~1.5 MB/iter (MALLOC_ARENA_MAX=1\nrules out glibc arenas), RSS flat-ish over hundreds of iters vs 6GB→OOM@80. Goldens unchanged (NUC textgold\n0.9999983, maskTest 0.9999997, int8 0.9920855; F32/int8 throughput unchanged ~60/~127 sent/s).\nLEAK-ORACLE LESSON: **the NUC (glibc) is the leak detector, NOT the Mac** — macOS libmalloc returns freed\npages so RSS stays bounded even WITH a leak (Mac looked fine at ~1.3GB the whole time); glibc retains, so a\ntrue leak shows as monotonic RSS growth that MALLOC_ARENA_MAX=1 does NOT flatten.\nRESIDUAL ~1.2 MB/iter (~36 KB/sentence) = a TRUE LEAK of HAXE HEAP OBJECTS, not tensors (all tensor frees\nverified complete): the `embedText` return `[for j in 0...dim]` array (bench discards it; a real server frees\nit after serialize), per-encode `mask`/`ids` arrays, `shape()` Array\u003cInt\u003e results, WordPiece tokenization\nstrings/arrays. **jemalloc PROVES it's a real leak not retention**: LD_PRELOAD=libjemalloc.so.2 +\n`MALLOC_CONF=dirty_decay_ms:0,muzzy_decay_ms:0` (returns freed pages immediately) only trimmed 1.5→1.17 MB/iter,\nstill linear growth (jemalloc works now — the old \"can't resolve symbol alloc\" is gone). USE_JEMALLOC toggle =\nrun_bundle.sh/bench_server.sh maybe_enable_jemalloc (LD_PRELOAD /usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libjemalloc.so.2).\nThis residual is the insert_free.rs \"inner-block allocs: skip for now\" gap (line 336) — a DIFFERENT, broader\nclass than tensor management: freeing loop-body Haxe allocs at last-use is unsound for loop-CARRIED ones (UAF\nnext iter) but SAFE for iteration-local ones; current code skips all inner-block conservatively. Closing it =\ndistinguish loop-carried (phi) vs iteration-local in insert_free — the hard general fix, not BERT-specific.\nSEPARATE compiler bug found: `for (it in 0...iters)` with a VARIABLE Int bound doesn't terminate (benchMode ran\nunbounded for iters=12/20/40 alike; null-guard on parseInt didn't fix it).\n\nThe same InsertFreePass gap bites BERT (all-MiniLM-L6). Measured on the NUC via a bench-loop that\nrepeatedly calls `embedText` (discards the result — so the leak is the ENCODE INTERNALS, not the\nreturned `Array\u003cFloat\u003e`): single process RSS climbs **~70 MB per 32-sentence iter ≈ 2.2 MB/sentence**,\n450 MB (model loaded) → **~6 GB → process dies (OOM) on the 8 GB NUC** before completing. Freed fully on\nexit (per-process, not a runtime-wide leak). Short runs survive (the 128-encode bench_embed grew only\n~280 MB, ran clean at 56 sent/s); a PERSISTENT embed-server would balloon to OOM — competition-relevant.\n- **NOT boxing**: MIR `box_float`/`box_int` = 0 calls in the encode → the [[bugs_float_conditional_reassign_boxes]]\n  3-4GB pattern is NOT the cause here. It's transient activation TENSORS (attention/FFN/LayerNorm\n  intermediates: softmax, scale, matmulT outputs, addInto temporaries) not reaching `rayzor_tensor_free`.\n- **Pre-existing, dtype-agnostic**: F32 and int8 (NUE_INT8) leak equally (shared encode path); unrelated\n  to the int8 work (8adee996).\n- **Likely mechanism**: drops placed at FUNCTION scope, not LOOP-BODY scope — transient activations\n  accumulate across the per-sentence loop instead of freeing per-encode. Test the fix the same way:\n  RSS should stay flat across iters (bench mode in BertEmbedder, `bench \u003cgguf\u003e \u003csentences.txt\u003e \u003citers\u003e`).\n\nMIR→LLVM: all 8 `atomicrmw add ptr, i32 ±N seq_cst` live in `@workerLoop` / `@parallelRows` /\n`@matmulFused` (the threaded matmul pool path) — Arc refcount inc/dec. On x86 seq_cst atomicrmw =\n`lock xadd` (locks the refcount cache line). For BERT's small 384×384 matmuls, 8 pool workers hammering\none shared tensor's refcount → cache-line ping-pong; thread-coordination ≈ the tiny per-band compute\n(explains [[project_nuc_x86_target_thermal]] \"workers 6 = +1%\"). PERF lever, not correctness: relax the\nrefcount ordering (Relaxed inc / Acq-Rel dec) or don't thread matmuls this small. Distinct from the leak.\nNOTE: the BertEmbedder `bench` mode does NOT respect its iters arg (ran ~130 for a requested 12 — parseInt/\nrange bug); harness-only, made the leak vivid; the 2.15x int8 numbers came from the clean early iters.\n\nRelated: [[project-tensor-pool]], [[project-addinto-fused-kernel]], [[bugs-addinto-rebind-clone-drop-failure]], [[bugs-cleanup-scopes-frees-captures]], [[bugs-mark-escaping-incomplete]], [[project_nuc_x86_target_thermal]].\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_pool_data_flow_gap` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}